# AGENTS.md

This file guides AI coding agents working in this repository.

{{ solution_name }} is built on the [nArchitecture](https://github.com/kodlamaio-projects/nArchitecture) starter: a Clean
Architecture monolith with CQRS on `NArchitecture.Core.Mediator`, Mapperly mappers and the `NArchitecture.Core.*` packages.
Feature code is generated with [nArchGen](https://github.com/kodlamaio-projects/nArchitecture.Gen); use it instead of
writing that boilerplate by hand.

## Commands

```bash
dotnet restore
dotnet build
{{~ if has_tests ~}}
dotnet test
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if has_tool_manifest ~}}
dotnet tool restore{{ if has_csharpier }}
dotnet csharpier format .{{ end }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if project ~}}
dotnet run --project src/{{ project.name }}/WebAPI
{{~ end ~}}
```

## Layout

{{~ if (projects | array.size) > 1 ~}}
Projects under `src/`: {{ for p in projects }}`{{ p.name }}`{{ if !for.last }}, {{ end }}{{ end }}. Each has the layers below.
{{~ else if project ~}}
The application lives in `src/{{ project.name }}/`.
{{~ end ~}}

- `Domain/Entities` – entities, deriving from `Entity<TId>`.
- `Application/Features/<PluralEntity>/` – one folder per feature:
  - `Commands/<Name>/`, `Queries/<Name>/` – the request with its handler as a nested class, the response and a
    FluentValidation validator.
  - `Constants/` – operation claim names and business message keys.
  - `Mappers/` – the Mapperly mapper of the feature.
  - `Rules/` – business rules, deriving from `BaseBusinessRules`.
  - `Resources/Locales/` – localized messages (`<Feature>.en.yaml`).
- `Application/Services/` – services; repository interfaces are in `Services/Repositories`.
- `Persistence/` – `Contexts` (DbContexts), `EntityConfigurations`, `Repositories`.
- `Infrastructure/` – adapters for external services.
- `WebAPI/Controllers` – controllers deriving from `BaseController`; they only send requests with `Mediator.Send`.
{{~ if has_tests ~}}
- `tests/` – test projects.
{{~ end ~}}

## Conventions

- Requests implement `IRequest<TResponse>` and handlers `IRequestHandler<TRequest, TResponse>` from
  `NArchitecture.Core.Mediator`. Do not add MediatR.
- Map with Mapperly (`[Mapper]` static partial classes in `Mappers/`). Do not add AutoMapper.
- Cross-cutting behavior is switched on by interfaces on the request: `ISecuredRequest` (roles), `ICachableRequest` /
  `ICacheRemoverRequest`, `ILoggableRequest`, `ITransactionalRequest`.
- Business errors are thrown from the feature's `*BusinessRules` class. Message keys live in
  `Constants/*BusinessMessages.cs`, their texts in `Resources/Locales`.
- Keep controllers thin: no business logic outside the Application layer.

## Generating code with nArchGen

Run nArchGen from the repository root{{ if is_generator_local_tool }}. It is installed as a local tool: run
`dotnet tool restore` once, then call it as `dotnet nArchGen`{{ end }}.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| CRUD for a new entity | Write `Domain/Entities/<Entity>.cs` first, then `{{ generator_command }} generate crud <Entity> <DbContext>` |
| Command in a feature | `{{ generator_command }} generate command <Name> <PluralFeature> -e Post\|Put\|Delete` |
| Query in a feature | `{{ generator_command }} generate query <Name> <PluralFeature>` |
| Dynamic query for an entity | `{{ generator_command }} generate dynamic-query <Entity>` |

`generate crud` options: `-c` caching, `-l` logging, `-t` transaction, `-s` secured (operation claims), `-d` dynamic
query. Unset options stay off.

{{~ for p in projects ~}}
{{~ if (p.db_contexts | array.size) > 0 ~}}
DbContexts{{ if (projects | array.size) > 1 }} of `{{ p.name }}`{{ end }}: {{ for c in p.db_contexts }}`{{ c }}`{{ if !for.last }}, {{ end }}{{ end }}.
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if (projects | array.size) > 1 ~}}
Pass `--project <name>` to pick the project under `src/`.
{{~ end ~}}

Run it like this:

1. Preview first: add `--dry-run --json`. Nothing is written; `details.plannedCreated`, `details.plannedUpdated` and
   `details.diffs` show what would change.
2. Run the same command without `--dry-run`. `created` and `updated` list the files it wrote.
3. Read the exit code:

   | Code | Meaning | What to do |
   |---|---|---|
   | `0` | Success | Build, then fill in the business logic. |
   | `1` | A business rule failed | Report `error.message` to the user. |
   | `2` | Missing or invalid argument | Fix the arguments from `error.message` and run again. |
   | `3` | Files already exist, nothing written | Ask the user before running again with `--force`. |
   | `4` | Unexpected error | Stop and report it. |

4. Never pass `--force` without the user's consent: it overwrites the existing files.

`--json` never prompts. Without it, pass `--no-interactive` so that a missing value fails instead of waiting for input.
{{~ if (tool_files | array.size) > 0 || is_codex_selected ~}}

